Intermittent mild hoarseness following a recent self-resolved upper respiratory tract infection
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Rough raspy vocal quality, Vocal fatigue, Recent URI history

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-009
Reason for Visit: Persistent change in voice quality causing anxiety regarding airway compromise or laryngeal pathology.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an altered voice and persistent throat discomfort following a URI. Urgent clinical assessment was mandated to evaluate the airway, rule out acute epiglottitis or impending airway obstruction, and perform a thorough head and neck examination prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My cold is gone but my voice is rough and keeps giving out by the evening.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a rough raspy vocal quality persisting for 5 days after resolution of acute nasal symptoms from a URI. Describes vocal fatigue worsening towards the end of the day. Notes frequent throat clearing without productive phlegm and a mild tickling sensation in the laryngeal region. Denies shortness of breath. Complete resolution of initial coryzal symptoms. Absence of stridor or respiratory distress.
Functional Status: Fully independent, but limiting social conversation.
Social/Safety Concerns: Anxiety regarding potential permanent vocal cord damage.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/76 | HR: 72 | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 14 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Alert, breathing comfortably on room air, phonating with a raspy quality.
Physical Exam: Respiratory exam shows clear lung fields bilaterally with no stridor or wheeze. Normal anterior neck examination without palpable masses or cervical lymphadenopathy. Oropharynx is clear without exudate or severe erythema. Normal swallowing mechanism observed. Diagnostic triad confirmed: rough raspy vocal quality, vocal fatigue, and a recent URI history. Laryngoscopy would be expected to show mild bilateral vocal fold erythema and edema.
Environmental Observations: Dry indoor environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Frequent use of over-the-counter throat lozenges.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Intermittent mild hoarseness following a recent self-resolved upper respiratory tract infection (Post-viral laryngitis) - Status: Improving
Differentials:

Acute Epiglottitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of high fever, drooling, stridor, or toxic appearance.

Laryngeal Malignancy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Acute onset directly following a clear viral upper respiratory infection, and lack of weight loss or hemoptysis.

Vocal Cord Nodules - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Acute onset rather than chronic progressive hoarseness linked to long-term vocal abuse.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The presentation of altered phonation and throat discomfort necessitates careful evaluation to exclude critical airway threats. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively rule out stridor, assess work of breathing, and exclude signs of supraglottic swelling (such as epiglottitis), which can rapidly progress to airway obstruction. Clinical examination confirming a stable airway and attributing symptoms to post-viral vocal fold edema safely avoided unnecessary ED referral while providing crucial reassurance.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance. Advice on strict vocal rest and aggressive oral hydration. Planned non-urgent flexible laryngoscopy only if symptoms persist beyond 3 weeks.
Positive Behavioral Change: Substitute habitual throat clearing, which forcefully slams inflamed vocal cords together, with taking a small sip of water to clear mucus gently.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Use of a cool mist humidifier at night.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks if hoarseness has not fully resolved.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if you develop sudden difficulty breathing, noisy breathing (stridor), inability to swallow your own saliva, or a high fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; persistent hoarseness beyond 3-4 weeks requires direct visualization of the vocal cords by an ENT specialist.
Clinical Course: Post-viral laryngeal edema typically resolves within 10 to 14 days with adequate vocal rest and hydration.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if breathing becomes difficult, if you start coughing up blood, or if swallowing becomes acutely painful.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The viral infection caused the vocal cords to swell. Just like a sprained ankle, they need rest to reduce the swelling. Whispering or shouting strains them further.
Actionable Steps: Maintain strict vocal rest—speak normally but only when absolutely necessary. Do not whisper. Drink 2 liters of water daily.

